have you ever heard of a company called  ge  you might not know them by name but i'm  sure you recognize their logo they're  that company that makes washers dryers  and refrigerators but did you know that  they were the world's largest company  not too long ago  in fact it was just 13 years ago that  they reached the top of the fortune 500.  yet today ge can't even turn a profit  it's actually been seven years since  they were consistently profitable  this has forced them to liquidate dozens  of subsidiaries at rock bottom prices  and reduce their workforce by over a  hundred thousand employees as recent as  2017 ge boasted over 300 000 employees  but today they only have about 168 000  all of this has cost the ge stock to  tumble 85 percent from their peak  shedding nearly 400 billion dollars in  market cap things have gotten so bad  that ge resorted to doing a reverse talk  split of 1 to 8 just last year and if  you weren't already convinced that ge  was getting destroyed just listen to  this  ge was the original member of the dow  jones and they have continuously been  part of the index since 1907 but in 2018  the dow jones kicked out ge  i'm not sure they could have been  insulted it any more than this  so here's how ge they priced a  multi-hundred billion fortune before a  newborn could become a teenager  while g's fundamental crisis didn't  become evident until the last 10 years  ge has actually been making bad moves  for over 40 years it all started in 1981  when a man named jack welch became the  ceo of the company jack came from a  rather humble family his father was a  regular old railroad conductor while his  mother was a homemaker this meant that  throughout his childhood he worked  several odd jobs including being a golf  caddy a newspaper delivery boy a shoe  salesman and a drill press operator and  when he joined the ge in 1916 he was  simply a junior chemical engineer  considering this blue collar background  you would think that jack was  sympathetic to the struggles of everyday  americans and that he would treat his  employees really well but jack was  actually the exact opposite he was  ruthless rash and rather heartless in  fact by the time jack became a  well-known businessman most people would  come neutron jack because he was like a  neutron bomb but wait a minute what did  jack even do to receive such a title  well he was basically the mastermind  behind all of the worst parts of  capitalism for example he was the one  that popularized the rank and the inc  policy this is when you fire the bottom  10 percent of your workforce every  single year if this sounds familiar to  you it's probably because you've heard  of amazon adopting the same policy while  this strategy might sound reasonable at  first glance it leads to terrible  long-term performance here's the thing  when you're constantly firing the bottom  10 percent of your workforce your  employees are never able to focus on the  big picture they're always worried about  surviving next year's cuts this was  especially true amongst management which  led to cooking the books  now they weren't doing anything illegal  per se at least at the beginning but  they were manipulating the numbers  through every loophole they could find  for example let's say you were the  manager of a department at ge and your  goal is to achieve a million dollars in  profit this quarter  last quarter this department only pulled  a nine hundred thousand dollars in  profit so you have to increase profit by  roughly 10 percent ideally you would  look for ways to grow the revenue that  your department brought in but this  would take multiple quarters if not  years  so instead you decide to simply fire 10  employees that are being paid a few  thousand a month this allows your  department to beat the vein metric of a  million dollars in profit however your  department is nowhere near as strong as  a one million dollar department should  be and this wasn't just happening here  and there it was happening company wide  from the top down and jack was the most  infamous for it he didn't bolster the  company's profits by firing people but  he did boast the company's balance sheet  by firing people  for example let's say you want to make a  billion dollar acquisition you could  wait a few years for the company to  build up sufficient cash reserves or you  could just fire five percent of your  workforce and use their payrolls to fund  the purchase and that's exactly what  jack did it throughout his tenure  jack was only ceo for 20 years but  within this time he made 600  acquisitions  this works out to 30 acquisitions per  year or two and a half acquisitions  every single month  obviously this is by no means naturally  sustainable looking back it's obvious  why ge eventually got destroyed but at  the time it looked like ge was crushing  it given jack's strict department-wide  goals the company would always beat wall  street's earnings expectations by at  least one cent per share  on top of this their assets were growing  exponentially thanks to all of the  acquisitions which sent the company's  stock to the moon when jack took over in  1981 the company was only worth 14  billion dollars but when he left in 2001  it was worth 410 billion dollars but  jack didn't build a true 410 billion  dollar company what he built was a  machine that could consistently top wall  street's expectations also the company  was not filled with loyal employees who  were excited to build up the company  instead it was basically filled with  contractors who are good at playing the  purging game this toxic culture was  synonymous with ge by the time jack left  and though the company was doing well on  paper is fundamentally weaker than ever  before  and it just took one economic downturn  to topple the house of cards  by now i'm sure you're all familiar with  what happened in 2008 the great  financial recession now you might think  that ge was relatively safe from this  given that they mainly deal with  physical products like appliances so  while sales could go down and there were  no risk of facing a real crisis right  well this would have been the case if  ges stuck to their original business but  jack had expanded ge into basically  every industry you can think of  including the financial industry their  financial subsidiary was called the ge  capital and they were the fifth largest  lender in the us  given what happened to lenders in 2008  this was already a terrible position to  be in but this was just half the story  you see while many banks overextended  themselves and engaged in self-prime  lending at least they had deposits they  just landed out these deposits in bad  ways  ge however didn't even have deposits  they borrowed money through commercial  papers at low interest rates and then  lended that money to people at higher  interest rates ge even bragged about  being the largest borrower of a  commercial paper  at the time they accounted for four  percent of all commercial paper in the  us for perspective g's entire debt load  translated to over half a trillion  dollars personally i don't think this  was something to brag about because when  the financial crisis hit they were  screwed on both ends they couldn't  collect payments from borrowers nor  could they pay off their own debts and  pretty soon they needed a bailout now i  know a lot of you guys love to criticize  the big banks for getting bailouts of 10  to 30 billion dollars but all of that is  nothing in comparison to what ge got ge  didn't get a bailout worth 10 20 or even  50 billion dollars their bailout was  worth the disgusting of 139  billion dollars  so if anyone deserves criticism for  bailouts it's these guys this bailout  didn't really help turn around the  company either as the company was  already rotten to the core it basically  just ensured that the company didn't go  under in a matter of weeks  ever since the bailout ge has only been  on a decline regardless of what metric  you look at they've literally only  gotten destroyed  their assets are down from 781 billion  dollars to 185 billion dollars their  cash reserves are down from 120 billion  dollars to 20 billion  their revenue is down from 150 billion  to 75 billion  and as for their net income well it's  not even positive this isn't that  surprising given that ge has just been  downsizing it ever since the bailout ge  has basically sold everything even  including their appliance business so if  you bought a ge appliance in the past  six years it wasn't even made by ge  themselves it was actually made by a  chinese company called hair who has  rights to the ge brand  at this point ge themselves are only  involved in three industries which are  aviation healthcare and energy and ge is  planning on spinning these off into  three different companies as well  the healthcare spin-off is supposed to  take place in early 2023 and the energy  spin-off is supposed to take place in  early 2024  this means that the original ge will  only be left with the aviation industry  this is honestly quite a shame given how  large ge was just a decade ago the only  good thing that has happened to ge over  the past decade is their debt has also  gone down their long-term debt has  decreased from 336 billion dollars to 27  billion dollars similarly their total  liabilities have decreased from 656  billion dollars to 150 billion dollars  while this is far better than the  position that they used to be in it it's  still not all that great g's market cap  currently sits at 80 billion dollars  which means that the total liabilities  is still nearly twice their market cap  so ge still has quite a ways to go  in terms of what ge will look like after  the spin-offs it's just even more  disappointing in the past 6 months ge  pulled in a total revenue of 35 billion  dollars out of which 11.7 billion came  from the aerospace segment this means  that the aerospace segment roughly  accounts for a third of their business  given that their market cap is currently  80 billion dollars once the health care  and energy businesses are spun off the  market cap will be closer to 26 billion  dollars and 26 billion 202 dollars is  equivalent to just eight billion  nineteen ninety-one dollars when jack  took over in 1981 the company was worth  14 billion dollars which means that in  just two years ge will be worth less  than what they are worth way back in  1981.  now that's truly quite a tumble  despite ge's terrible current position  some people are hopeful that splitting  up and focusing on just one industry is  exactly what ge needs the aviation  industry is still very much recovering  from the pandemic slump so demand for  aircraft should only go up in the coming  years not to mention it g's other  segments are starting to make a  turnaround as well for example ge power  is finally profitable again and the  focus on renewable energy could pay off  big in the coming decades similarly as  supply chain issues finally cool down ge  healthcare is expected to boost margins  in the high teens so it looks like net  profitability is actually possible for  ge in the coming years but don't let  these short-term outlooks fool you sure  this could be the bottom for ge but it's  not like they're poised to launch the  iphone or become a leader in ai  really all they'll be doing is salvaging  their existing businesses and finally  making them profitable again in other  words they're just back to ground zero  their time at the top isn't helping them  in any way if anything it simply  burdened them with debt which they're  still trying to pay off so at this point  their chances of recovering to their  former glory is just as good as any  other company with their same market cap  in fact g's chances are probably worse  because of the remaining debt and lack  of momentum so what can we learn from  this well the takeaway seems to be that  the second a company starts to value  shareholders over customers they've  initiated their eventual demise they  might end up posting good numbers on  paper for years or even decades but  their fundamentally flawed approach does  eventually catch up and by the time they  realize it it's already way too late so  if you're a business owner make sure you  don't put profits over customers because  that's a surefire way to get screwed but  that's just what i think do you think ge  deserve their downfall comment that down  below  also drop a like if you're not a fan of  g's bailout and of course consider  checking out our discord community to  suggest future video ideas and consider  subscribing and see more questions  logically answered but until then  i'm hurry and i'll see you guys on the  next one

