 third of their business  given that their market cap is currently  80 billion dollars once the health care  and energy businesses are spun off the  market cap will be closer to 26 billion  dollars and 26 billion 202 dollars is  equivalent to just eight billion  nineteen ninety-one dollars when jack  took over in 1981 the company was worth  14 billion dollars which means that in  just two years ge will be worth less  than what they are worth way back in  1981.  now that's truly quite a tumble  despite ge's terrible current position  some people are hopeful that splitting  up and focusing on just one industry is  exactly what ge needs the aviation  industry is still very much recovering  from the pandemic slump so demand for  aircraft should only go up in the coming  years not to mention it g's other  segments are starting to make a  turnaround as well for example ge power  is finally profitable again and the  focus on renewable energy could pay off  big in the coming decades similarly as  supply chain issues finally cool down ge  healthcare is expected to boost margins  in the high teens so it looks like net  profitability is actually possible for  ge in the coming years but don't let  these short-term outlooks fool you sure  this could be the bottom for ge but it's  not like they're poised to launch the  iphone or become a leader in ai  really all they'll be doing is salvaging  their existing businesses and finally  making them profitable again in other  words they're just back to ground zero  their time at the top isn't helping them  in any way if anything it simply  burdened them with debt which they're  still trying to pay off so at this point  their chances of recovering to their  former glory is just as good as any  other company with their same market cap  in fact g's chances are probably worse  because of the remaining debt and lack  of momentum so what can we learn from  this well the takeaway seems to be that  the second a company starts to value  shareholders over customers they've  initiated their eventual demise they  might end up posting good numbers on  paper for years or even decades but  their fundamentally flawed approach does  eventually catch up and by the time they  realize it it's already way too late so  if you're a business owner make sure you  don't put profits over customers because  that's a surefire way to get screwed but  that's just what i think do you think ge  deserve their downfall comment that down  below  also drop a like if you're not a fan of  g's bailout and of course consider  checking out our discord community to  suggest future video ideas and consider  subscribing and see more questions  logically answered but until then  i'm hurry and i'll see you guys on the  next one


